To solve this problem, we can use dynamic programming to count the number of good strings. We'll create a table dp where dp[i][j] represents the number of palindromic Hamiltonian paths ending on vertex i with the last character being j.

The base case is dp[1][a] = 1 for each a in the first k letters, since there's exactly one way to start on any letter of the alphabet.

Then for each intermediate vertex, dp[i][j] is the sum of dp[i][k] for all k such that i to k or k to i form a valid palindromic path.

After filling the dp table, we sum up dp[n][a] for all a to get the total number of good strings.

